## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end streaming ingestion, lightweight validation, data quality scoring, and practical cost governance in a beginner-friendly setup.

## Key Concepts
- Kinesis Data Streams + Lambda ingestion
- Lightweight JSON validation and 0–100 data quality scoring
- Parquet storage in S3 with region/date partitioning
- DLQ handling for invalid records
- DynamoDB for regional budgets and a guard lambda to pause ingestion
- Lifecycle policies for cost control; CloudWatch observability

## Code Example
```javascript
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  for (const r of event.Records) {
    const payload = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(r.kinesis.data, 'base64').toString('utf8'));
    const ok = validate(payload);
    if (!ok) {
      await sendToDLQ(payload);
      continue;
    }
    const score = computeQuality(payload);
    await writeParquetToS3(payload, score);
  }
  return { status: 'ok' };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test schema evolution and optional fields in this pipeline?
- How would you validate and recover from late data while respecting the budget guard?
- What monitoring dashboards would you build to track data quality, cost, and DLQ trends?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a secure, end-to-end streaming pipeline with encryption, data redaction, and reliable error handling.

## Key Concepts
- AWS KMS and the AWS Encryption SDK for per-record encryption
- PII data redaction techniques
- Parquet format in S3 with date-based partitioning
- Dead Letter Queue (DLQ) design and routing patterns
- X-Ray distributed tracing across producer, validator, and sink services
- LocalStack for local testing and development

## Code Example
```javascript
// Decrypt and process Kinesis record
const decrypted = encryptionClient.decrypt(encryptedPayload, dataKey);

// Validate telemetry schema
if (!schemaValidator.validate(decrypted)) {
  await dlqClient.send(JSON.stringify(decrypted));
  return;
}

// Redact PII fields
const redacted = piiRedactor.redact(decrypted);

// Write to S3 as partitioned Parquet
await s3Client.putObject({
  Bucket: 'telemetry-bucket',
  Key: `year=${date.getFullYear()}/month=${date.getMonth()}/day=${date.getDate()}/${uuid()}.parquet`,
  Body: await parquetWriter.write(redacted)
});
```

